abstract:
Background: Clinical calculators are widely used in critical care and perioperative medicine to support risk stratification, prognosis, and treatment planning. Although web-based tools have improved access, these calculators still require manual entry of multiple clinical variables, which can be cumbersome and may introduce errors. Large language models (LLMs) may offer an alternative by automating clinical calculation tasks through natural-language prompting. However, the accuracy of clinician-facing LLMs for routine clinical calculation tasks remains poorly characterized.&amp;nbsp; 
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Methods: Four clinician-facing LLMs (ChatGPT-5.2, Gemini-3, Claude Haiku-4.5, and DeepSeek-V3.2) and one LLM-integrated evidence retrieval platform (OpenEvidence) were evaluated by prompting them to use one of 30 clinical calculators. Calculators were categorized as classification-based, simple additive, weighted additive, or equation-based. Five standardized prompts per calculator were tested, and outputs were compared with reference values from MDCalc. Accuracy and error classification were analyzed.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Results: ChatGPT-5.2 achieved the highest accuracy (95.3%) and significantly outperformed the other evaluated models (p &amp;lt; 0.001), including OpenEvidence (72.7%), Gemini-3 (66.7%), Claude Haiku-4.5 (39.3%), and DeepSeek-V3.2 (32.0%). Weighted additive calculators were the most challenging, whereas classification and equation-based tools performed better. Errors were more often underestimations, and most additive errors preserved correct clinical stratification.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Conclusions: Clinician-facing LLMs showed highly variable accuracy across clinical calculators. While ChatGPT-5.2 performed best, no model demonstrated sufficient reliability for unsupervised clinical use. Safer near-term applications may involve using LLMs to interface with validated external calculators rather than relying solely on intrinsic model computation.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The rising utilization of generative artificial intelligence ("AI") techniques in legal writing has engendered a novel category of professional accountability issues. Courts around the United States have seen instances where attorneys presented pleadings that included legal authority produced by AI systems that were either fictitious or misrepresented. In several cases, attorneys utilized AI-generated case citations without confirming their authenticity. The outcome has been the submission of briefs that include spurious instances, invented quotations, or accounts of precedent that are absent from the referenced authority. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Judicial bodies have reacted by enacting punishments, submitting attorneys to disciplinary agencies, and underscoring that the use of AI does not absolve lawyers of their duty to authenticate the authenticity of the legal precedents mentioned in their submissions. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Simultaneously, courts and commentators have acknowledged a similar although separate issue: generative AI systems often misrepresent the holdings of genuine cases, erroneously assigning legal propositions to instances that do not support such notions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This article examines the principal decisions concerning two types of AI-related legal errors: (1) penalties for presenting fabricated AI-generated case citations and (2) occurrences where AI tools misrepresent the contents of authentic cases. It culminates with suggestions on how Maryland courts ought to tackle this burgeoning issue.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
